First, a full browser page reload is incorrectly triggering a live refresh of the widget. On mount, the widget should only rehydrate from the persisted/shared cache, never trigger a new job run. A live refresh should happen only in two cases: when the auto-refresh timer interval elapses, or when the user explicitly clicks the widget’s own Refresh button. A browser-level reload (F5, tab reload) must not be treated as a trigger. If the auto-refresh timer’s remaining time is not itself persisted across a reload, do not let the reset timer immediately fire a refresh either — resume or restart the countdown cleanly, without dispatching an immediate fetch.

Second, the status job this widget uses to check pod counts is shared across all applications, but it runs sequentially instead of in parallel: on a given refresh, only one application’s job actually executes, and the others are queued or skipped, requiring further refresh cycles before each remaining application updates. Change the job orchestration so the same job type can execute concurrently for different applications: track each run with a key based on both the job identifier and the application/item identifier, rather than a single lock per job identifier alone. Different applications must be free to run this job at the same time. However, the same job for the same specific application must still guard against being triggered twice concurrently — for example a second user triggering it, or the widget’s auto and manual refresh overlapping. In that case, do not start a duplicate run; surface a message that the job is already running for that application, and point the caller at the in-progress run instead.

Third, when an application shows a load failed state, the widget gives no indication of how stale the underlying data is. Show the timestamp of when that application’s data was last loaded successfully, since different applications can fail and go stale by different amounts of time. Source this from the persisted cache’s last-successful-load time for that specific application, independent of the other applications’ timestamps.
